Quick sanity check on stability before handing over code: with the phone's center of mass leaning back at 30° off vertical (60° from horizontal) against an ~80–90 mm support, and the lip set 70 mm back from the front edge, the combined center of gravity lands well inside the footprint — no tip-forward or tip-backward risk. Numbers check out, so here's the model.

```openscad
// Phone stand — PETG, 80 mm wide, 3 mm walls
// Back support tilted 60° from horizontal, 10 mm front lip

width        = 80;   // stand width (Y axis)
wall         = 3;    // wall thickness
tilt_angle   = 60;   // back support angle from horizontal, degrees
base_depth   = 70;   // front-to-back footprint depth
lip_height   = 10;   // front lip height above the base
slot_gap     = 11;   // gap between lip and back support (phone + case thickness)
panel_length = 90;   // back support slant length

eps = 0.01; // overlap so unions don't leave coincident faces

module base_plate() {
    cube([base_depth, width, wall]);
}

module front_lip() {
    cube([wall, width, wall + lip_height]);
}

module back_support() {
    translate([wall + slot_gap, 0, wall - eps])
        rotate([0, -tilt_angle, 0])
            cube([panel_length, width, wall]);
}

union() {
    base_plate();
    front_lip();
    back_support();
}
```

Modeled in its natural "in-use" orientation (base flat, lip forward) so it's easy to read and tweak. For printing, use your slicer's "place on face" / "lay flat" tool and select the back support's outer face — that lays the part on its side with zero overhangs and a full first layer, matching the 60° tilt you confirmed.

Adjust `base_depth`, `panel_length`, or `slot_gap` if you want a snugger fit for a specific phone+case combo.

Here's the OpenSCAD code, built directly in the print-ready orientation (lying on its side — the tilted support and lip are entirely captured within each 2D layer, so it extrudes straight up with no overhangs and no supports needed):

```openscad
// ============================================================
// Phone stand — PETG, 3 mm walls
// Modeled directly in the as-printed orientation: the side
// profile (base + lip + angled support) is extruded along Z,
// which becomes the stand's WIDTH. Each print layer is the
// full profile, so it lies flat on the bed with no overhangs.
// ============================================================

width       = 80;   // stand width == print build height (Z)
wall        = 3;    // wall thickness used throughout
base_depth  = 60;   // front-to-back footprint of the base
lip_height  = 10;   // lip height above the base top surface
support_len = 90;   // length of the angled back support
tilt_angle  = 60;   // support angle, measured from horizontal

preview_upright = false; // true: rotate upright, for viewing only
                         // false: leave as-printed (lying on its side)

module profile_2d() {
    tip_x = -support_len * cos(tilt_angle);
    tip_y = wall + support_len * sin(tilt_angle);

    union() {
        // base plate
        square([base_depth, wall]);

        // front lip — catches the bottom edge of the phone
        translate([base_depth - wall, wall])
            square([wall, lip_height]);

        // back support — riser seated on the base, blended up
        // to the tip of the angled bar
        hull() {
            translate([0, 0])
                square([wall, wall * 2]);
            translate([tip_x, tip_y])
                circle(d = wall, $fn = 24);
        }
    }
}

module phone_stand() {
    linear_extrude(height = width)
        profile_2d();
}

if (preview_upright) {
    rotate([90, 0, 0])
        phone_stand();
} else {
    phone_stand();
}
```

Notes:
- Default dimensions give roughly an 80×105×85 mm stand (W×D×H) — adjust `base_depth`/`support_len` if you want it more compact.
- With `preview_upright = false` (default), the exported STL is already in the correct orientation to slice — no rotation needed in your slicer.
- Set `preview_upright = true` temporarily if you want to eyeball it standing up in the OpenSCAD viewer before printing.
- The support attaches via a small `hull()`-blended riser rather than a knife-edge joint, so it's a real bonded connection, not just touching at a point.

